<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<!-- Generated by Pandoc using pretext.lua -->
<section xml:id="sec-rxns6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Moles</title>

	<p>
		In chemistry, we often have to deal with very large numbers of things (ex.
		atoms or molecules).
		For convenience, we can rely of scientific notation to help us out.
		We can also use the term mole to help us out.
		A mole is a word that represents a number of things (similar to how a dozen
		means 12 things).
		The number represented by a mole is Avogadro’s number and has a value of <m>6.022 \times 10^{23}</m> .
		Why this weird number?
		You’ll have to wait until the next section of this lesson to find out.
		We can use the definition of a mole as a conversion factor to convert between
		numbers of things (atoms/molecules/etc.) and moles:
	</p>

	<p>
		<m>1\ \text{mole}</m> <m>6.022 \times 10^{23}</m> or <m>6.022 \times 10^{23}</m> <m>1\ \text{mole}</m> When carrying out calculations
		involving numbers of molecules and/or atoms, it is VERY important to add
		labels to our units to specify what is being quantified.
		This can help us see if we need to use other conversion factors in our
		problems.
		See the example below.
	</p>

	<p>
		How many molecules are in <m>3.0\ \text{moles}</m> of water?
		How many hydrogen atoms are in <m>3.0\ \text{moles}</m> of water?
	</p>

	<p>
		1.
		We begin with <m>3.0\ \text{moles}</m> of water and want to determine the number of molecules
		of water.
		To do this we need to use Avogadro’s number as shown below: <m>6.022 \times 10^{23}</m>
		molecules <m>3.0\ \text{moles}</m> <m>H_2O</m> ( ) = <m>1.8 \times 10^{24}</m> molecules <m>H_2O</m> <m>1\ \text{mole}</m> 2.
		To convert from molecules of water to number of hydrogen atoms, we need to see
		that 1 water molecule contains 2 hydrogen atoms.
		This can be written as a conversion factor and used to solve the problem as
		shown: 2 hydrogen atoms <m>1.8 \times 10^{24}</m> molecules <m>H_2O</m> ( ) = <m>3.6 \times 10^{24}</m> hydrogen
		atoms <m>1\ \text{molecule}</m> <m>H_2O</m>
	</p>

	<p>
		Hint to try and understand moles:
	</p>

	<p>
		When faced with confusing concepts in chemistry, like a mole, it can sometimes
		be helpful to think of more familiar examples with simpler numbers.
		Recall that a mole is like a dozen; it is a word that represents a number.
	</p>

	<p>
		If you have 2 dozen eggs, how many do you have?
		24 of course.
		How did you get that?
		You took the 2 and multiplied it by the number represented by a dozen (12).
	</p>

	<p>
		So if you have <m>2\ \text{moles}</m> of molecules, you’ll follow the same pattern: 2 times
		the number represented by a mole (<m>6.022 \times 10^{23}</m>).
	</p>

	<p>
		Likewise if I have 48 eggs, how many dozen do I have?
		4 dozen.
		You get this by taking the number of objects and dividing by the number
		represented by a dozen.
	</p>

	<p>
		So...how would you determine how many moles of molecules you have if you have
		<m>432\ \text{molecules}</m>?
		Take that number and divide it by the number represented by a mole.
	</p>
	<exercises>
		<title>Exercise 3</title>

		<exercise>
			<statement>
				<p>
					How many molecules are in <m>0.366\ \text{moles}</m> of carbon dioxide?
					How many carbon atoms?
					How many atoms?
					How many protons?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>2.20 \times 10^{23}</m> molecules, <m>2.20 \times 10^{23}</m> carbon atoms, <m>6.61 \times 10^{23}</m> atoms, <m>4.85 \times 10^{24}</m> protons
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise>
			<statement>
				<p>
					If you have <m>7.33 \times 10^{26}</m> hydrogen atoms, then how many moles of atoms do you
					have.
					If those hydrogen atoms are in the form of <m>H_2</m> , then how many molecules of
					<m>H_2</m> do you have?
					How many moles of <m>H_2</m> molecules do you have?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>1220\ \text{moles}</m> H atoms, <m>3.67 \times 10^{26}</m> H atoms, <m>609\ \text{moles}</m> <m>H_2</m> molecules
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>

		<exercise>
			<statement>
				<p>
					If <m>3.0\ \text{moles}</m> of carbon dioxide are mixed with <m>4.5\ \text{moles}</m> of water, how many
					moles of oxygen atoms are there?
					How many atoms of oxygen?
				</p>
			</statement>

			<answer>
				<p>
					<m>10.5\ \text{moles}</m> oxygen atoms, <m>6.32 \times 10^{24}</m> atoms of oxygen
				</p>
			</answer>
		</exercise>
	</exercises>
</section>
